form_visual_language:
  status: MASTER_SYNCED
  intended_master_path: form_visual_language.HYDE
  scope: FORM_HYDE_ONLY
  Jekyll_rule: K1 defines no form-specific palette override for Jekyll. Jekyll uses the normal authored scene rendering produced
    by the manuscript, AuthorWeather and other global presentation systems.
  Hyde:
    mode: CONSTRAINED_PREDATORY_PERCEPTION
    base_world:
      intent: near-monochrome or duotone treatment with strongly reduced ordinary color
      exact_palette: TBD_VISUAL_DIRECTION
      readability_rule: environment silhouettes, navigation and combat readability must remain intact
    semantic_accents:
      RED:
        intent: violence / blood / immediate physical threat
        eligibility: AUTHORED_TAG_ONLY
        example_tags:
        - HYDE_ACCENT_RED
      GOLD:
        intent: authored instinct-relevant object, target or cue
        eligibility: AUTHORED_TAG_ONLY
        example_tags:
        - HYDE_ACCENT_GOLD
    tagging_rule: Accent color is never inferred from generic interactability. Only authored/tagged objects or states may
      receive a semantic accent. A colored accent does not by itself create an interaction, clue, objective or branch.
    render_order:
    - resolve manuscript scene presentation including AuthorWeather / ManuscriptDistortion / ManuscriptOverdrive
    - render underlying authored scene
    - if Form == HYDE, apply Hyde base perception treatment
    - apply eligible semantic accent tags without changing their meaning
    Stevenson_interaction:
      AuthorWeather: continues to affect the underlying scene and atmosphere
      ManuscriptDistortion: may warp expression beneath/within the Hyde layer but must not redefine accent semantics
      ManuscriptOverdrive: may activate only declared authored visual/noise content; it must not automatically color every
        object or create false gameplay affordances
      ManuscriptFidelity: may change authored optional detail density; it does not remove the Hyde perception layer while
        Form == HYDE
    accessibility:
      color_alone_is_never_required: true
      secondary_cue: shape / luminance / outline / animation / audio as appropriate
    invariants:
    - Hyde perception changes presentation, not story topology.
    - Accent semantics remain stable across chapters unless the master explicitly changes them.
    - Mandatory information cannot rely on hue alone.
    - No untagged ordinary interactable receives an accent merely because it is interactable.
    - Exact LUT, post-process, emissive and material values are downstream visual implementation.
    K1_green_blood_rule:
      physical_color: GREEN
      semantic_accent_tag: NONE_BY_DEFAULT
      rule: K1 Hyde blood is a physical narrative evidence color and must remain recognizably green. Do not automatically
        recolor it with HYDE_ACCENT_RED. RED remains the semantic accent for authored violence/blood/threat targets that are
        explicitly tagged; the green self-blood clue is intentionally exceptional physical evidence.
      accessibility: 'Green hue is not the sole clue: unusual material/placement, knife tear and dialogue/context support
        recognition.'
